.TH DRAWDECODETEST 1
.SH NAME
drawdecodetest \- test hardware image decode into a draw image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drawdecodetest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Drawdecodetest
exercises
.IR draw (3)'s
.B video
file: decoding an encoded image directly into the pixels of a draw
image, using whatever hardware decoder the platform provides.
.PP
It allocates a 32-bit image, checks it really is blank, writes
.B "decode \fIid\fP \fIpath\fP"
to
.BR /dev/draw/\fIN\fP/video ,
reads the pixels back with an ordinary draw read, and requires the
result to be neither blank nor uniform.
Both checks matter: they are the two ways this fails quietly \(em
nothing written at all, and a solid fill from a decode that produced
nothing.
.PP
The image is
.B 65
pixels wide deliberately.
That is 260 bytes per row, which is not the aligned stride a texture
wants, so a texture-backed image (see
.IR draw (3)'s
.BR INFERNO_TEXIMAGE )
has a different
.B width
from the natural one.
That is precisely the case where a wrong
.B Memimage.width
or
.B .zero
puts every row at the wrong offset, and a decoded photograph read back
would show it.
Running with and without
.B INFERNO_TEXIMAGE
should give byte-identical results.
.PP
It speaks the draw protocol directly rather than using
.IR draw (2),
because the verb names its destination by image id and libdraw does
not expose ids to Limbo.
That is the right interface for the device \(em the id is how every
other draw message names an image \(em so the test uses it as libdraw
does, including binding
.B #i
into its own namespace first.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/drawdecodetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (3),
.IR draw (2)
.SH BUGS
Requires a platform with a hardware decoder, so it is meaningful only
under
.BR emu-cocoa .
Under
.B emu-g
there is no draw device at all and the test cannot even load
.IR draw (2).
.PP
It decodes one JPEG at one size.
It does not test other formats, other channel layouts, scaling to a
destination of a different aspect ratio, or a decode that fails \(em
and in particular it does not check the decoded pixels against a
reference decoder, only that they look like an image.
.IR readjpg (2)
exists in this tree and would make that comparison possible.
